\chapter{Cross-language conformance}
Two implementations are most useful when they share a behavioral contract.
Matching function names alone is insufficient: evaluation order, truth values,
integer boundaries, error recovery, and string indexing can all diverge.

\section{Use source-level fixtures}
The MuLL suite executes identical \api{.mull} files against both embeddings.
A successful fixture has a \api{.expected} output file. A negative fixture has
a \api{.error} file containing the required diagnostic fragment. Tests cover
aliasing, closure retention, recursion, precedence, short circuiting, package
state, formatting, invalid indexes, and malformed source.

Host-level tests exercise behavior that source fixtures cannot observe easily:
registration of callbacks, persistence between runs, source-size limits,
recovery after a step-budget error, and isolation between interpreter instances.
Toolkit regression tests separately cover whole-pattern matching and empty
source maps.

\section{Make documentation executable}
Complete MuLL listings in this book use the \api{mullcode} environment.
\api{scripts/check_mull.py} extracts each listing and executes it in a fresh
runtime. Both embeddings must produce the same output. Fragments that require
a host setup use the ordinary \api{code} environment instead.

When adding syntax, update the parser, both evaluators, a successful fixture,
an error fixture, and the corresponding reference chapter together. This keeps
the documented language smaller than or equal to the implemented language.
